#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/id #uuid "61bb0d60-a61e-5485-9df5-308e16dfd1aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5476" :question/problem "Determine if ![](images/5d/5da5dddce497b4b2b8ffe24924714e960a3c43088351e0a2a6d039000ed78035.svg) is an eigenvector of the matrix ![](images/6a/6a73978c951c4ae02d1455b5881a210c3d415bf1beef2f6f41f694c361708422.svg) If so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=4 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105621 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "f4ff15d3-1a6e-56c0-a7c4-13f18ef1a8e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5477" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](images/62/625c1f7a98060333fba9bf4e4172787a6860b796e6de90a12ac2ae4e21611275.svg) is an eigenvector of ![](images/34/34580c90a404ad28b7fb44eb2eebd0465e6cbb2a8bbd339d1981a7dfc8f84a29.svg) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $2.$\n\n- ![](images/20/2034a9bdc4a5f26258a8d72ff9496a72f98c9a9f755502318f2fd6ac0abc6553.svg) is an eigenvector of ![](images/60/60c921f21c6fc641a354cc91d398540c27f0e757bb59bc924f4f7ccaff11a731.svg) that corresponds to the eigenvalue $0.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = 5,$ then $2\\mathbf{v}$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda = 5.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A\\mathbf{v}=2 \\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. An eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector.\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(5v)=5(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2\\mathbf{v})=5( 2 \\mathbf{v} ),$ and $2 \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ this implies that $2 \\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda = 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n**Note:** Statement III is true in the general case, too. In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105622 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "6cba45d6-5a69-5b83-937c-c19368aaeea9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5478" :question/problem "An eigenvector of ![](images/3b/3b3b1d2eb2faaa02d5c758f820aa597ad2276365906ca16d701a42a0cdcda5af.svg) corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda=6c$ is  ![](images/7c/7c5842e4b605873d4d965b3d5d8a504ba8bb8610ed8e78d9df0dd186232f9e3b.svg) Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ such that $A\\mathbf{v}=\\lambda \\mathbf{v}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =6c[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2c \\\\ 1+4c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c \\\\ 12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2+2c=6c \\\\ 1+4c=12{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2+2c=6c \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+4c & =12{c}^{2} \\\\ 1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} & =12{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of the system is $c=\\dfrac{1}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105628 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "df40f37e-0d8f-5eb2-ad8a-9257440851df" :question/math-academy-id "e-5891" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdfa0528b5476ed0246679c45fd31824470063a68f33c4c9af0f714ad201538f.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $2.$ Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector ![](images/8d/8dd3f3b1b65f44a9d1db82d12e4dbf25e764d85c8bdfcd4538750a0e51c41d5d.svg) corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av=2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/78/78e1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17797bd9-5452-599e-90e2-f82db113737c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399540} {:answer/id #uuid "d97a9b83-e803-56f7-9eb3-247224ecca7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399541} {:answer/id #uuid "9d24ac33-bad1-5eef-bbbc-5c38f32cda9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399542} {:answer/id #uuid "dc542406-6a78-5728-b72b-ed2d72b9ad25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399543} {:answer/id #uuid "b6650df1-dfaf-53a5-afd8-f7fca95b0e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d97a9b83-e803-56f7-9eb3-247224ecca7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399541} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4725f6-2b66-51a3-bec8-dcfb0783e6bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56a4f3a9-ccca-53d2-8026-09ec5330fdbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-106775" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}16=2\\lambda  \\\\ -8=-4\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e9867f-15ba-5073-a20f-542045fced08" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalue that Corresponds to a Given Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a7b8b4-79e0-5a5e-b815-4fb73d16b52a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411273} {:answer/id #uuid "789dcf2a-206e-57f2-a9a3-9f05e8688d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411278} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3222d4-dee2-5a55-8149-170c56576786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411279} {:answer/id #uuid "b6451324-810e-5585-b20a-6b631b55ec25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411281} {:answer/id #uuid "eb0c0a14-6cf1-5cc9-93cf-929bd01a89bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "789dcf2a-206e-57f2-a9a3-9f05e8688d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411278} :answer-field/id #uuid "7db53a96-c2b8-5320-933c-98ee29c6736b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "075cd559-2719-533a-90f7-057ee06afb2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106776" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-10=5\\lambda  \\\\ -8=2\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cd35dae-4b0c-5adc-8bc4-a22cb37c512c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a3abd0ff1603a63a670252580ba7edb900b61e669aa12691ca912183c28899ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386231} {:answer/id #uuid "8e249e6a-38e6-53b3-a3c0-63a0fa0ffcee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e38721ff6fe7b75bef46079d0c877333a0113cbc782a12f61e09b832200943a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386232} {:answer/id #uuid "e5496c89-7d79-50b2-a456-45ec2257e5ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e4/e4ec74d60e6fdec5dec663ec4fe8f80ad9408817aa34afad916672a5221cdfad.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386233} {:answer/id #uuid "a69b8d1f-f466-5e97-aedb-fe58ec99359b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c59aa1f83fb6808cb6ea5ea2905c120b4b6c2af3a4e3850dc20d7f550f0259eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386234} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0fd56c-77d4-5a5e-81bc-85e9e915b29a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ae/aeb84eb023dcc22cc0a7b7713c5a78f76ba5469cd147471c99af6197d7806c30.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e249e6a-38e6-53b3-a3c0-63a0fa0ffcee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e38721ff6fe7b75bef46079d0c877333a0113cbc782a12f61e09b832200943a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386232} :answer-field/id #uuid "a20634f9-ffc3-50e6-810c-2409763e9ea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f38fa3e-b402-5304-8378-88160f430aba" :question/math-academy-id "q-106886" :question/problem "![](images/59/59bb15a433f4f9299a240fd0c4d03797425994a0aa17d69fa81cf92e6f99cabc.png)\n\nThe vector $u$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the vector $Bu.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector $u=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Bu & =\\frac{1}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/0e/0e38721ff6fe7b75bef46079d0c877333a0113cbc782a12f61e09b832200943a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11bca0a2-614b-50fd-87dc-eb967675e2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241333} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6f56e4-9815-5dda-9f18-707a058e88f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242038} {:answer/id #uuid "c5428e71-fdc1-5292-bb47-29f43f29145c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242919} {:answer/id #uuid "fd33897e-31a0-5885-b899-87b6d4853a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243412} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3a2465-d2fa-5709-98fd-7d830e35ecb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11bca0a2-614b-50fd-87dc-eb967675e2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241333} :answer-field/id #uuid "05e9ea84-8e38-5ba9-ae83-afa20ad22f9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcb8db9c-1fc6-56a1-9d1f-daa26c43ddf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-106946" :question/problem "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1-4c \\\\ -2+8c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1-4c=-3 \\\\ -2+8c=6c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-4c=-3\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2+8c=6c\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b2601b-5328-5b83-9985-c0e63b43e75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321290} {:answer/id #uuid "192c5006-34c0-5b1b-92a9-3b148e013f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321346} {:answer/id #uuid "393d9285-fba2-5766-833d-21bd3ee93339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321582} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba02f2e-1ea0-5ac2-bc42-4c65008f940e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321869} {:answer/id #uuid "966970c2-80d2-56c5-8f17-89142d065790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "192c5006-34c0-5b1b-92a9-3b148e013f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321346} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7f626c3-961d-5c1d-9776-1c7b61a81852" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bcdf208-8bdf-5d43-b755-321074d52b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37090" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $5$ then $10v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $5.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $6.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(10v)=10Av=10(5v)=5(10v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(10v)=5(10v),$ and $10v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $10v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=6v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =6.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "163f86d2-fdb8-5f21-84f8-737fdc9a2edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342433} {:answer/id #uuid "504eb653-acec-5ae9-a3f1-4c61020829f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342904} {:answer/id #uuid "64b87220-b079-5d53-b2c3-64bdc5bb99a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343092} {:answer/id #uuid "a94a82b6-33ed-590a-969a-6c33c2ea9547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343728} {:answer/id #uuid "dabe1242-62e5-5bc0-8259-75a8a4c362bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a94a82b6-33ed-590a-969a-6c33c2ea9547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343728} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e5d8ce-5e7d-508c-8cea-c2c668a22fc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e32f6312-bf7a-5fb4-bf70-bbf6dad31c7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75610" :question/problem "An eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]$ \ncorresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =5k-4$ is $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}] & =(5k-4)[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2(3k-1) \\\\ -10+8(3k-1)\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2(5k-4) \\\\ (5k-4)(3k-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6k \\\\ 24k-18\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10k-8 \\\\ 15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6k=10k-8 \\\\ 24k-18=15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n6k=10k-8\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24k-18 & =15{k}^{2}-17k+4 \\\\ 24\\cdot 2-18 & =15\\cdot {2}^{2}-17\\cdot 2+4 \\\\ 30 & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "273db6e4-b799-50bd-a66c-30f726dc163b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411274} {:answer/id #uuid "2add26dd-e43b-52c7-b2dd-74816713310b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411275} {:answer/id #uuid "4d09cdfe-9545-5f99-9a96-7ea92e64cc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411276} {:answer/id #uuid "5554e24d-43d6-558c-beb2-10fff427646c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411277} {:answer/id #uuid "a4868990-cd46-582c-81e2-72d1d4d50bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5554e24d-43d6-558c-beb2-10fff427646c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411277} :answer-field/id #uuid "e96c0df0-7687-56d8-909c-da1e58046f59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ddf6872-c9fc-59bb-a8a4-f3c287d11e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75724" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (but not $1$).\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4$ (but not $5$).\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(-3v)=-3(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2v)=-3(2v),$ and $2v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Product of a Matrix and an Eigenvector Given its Corresponding Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d82492-3855-57ba-b3fa-259a3e6e9203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c0/c0bffb825fa5610288e3c88555a41c8124cf3538b2f75ebe627543a678ca3fba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393558} {:answer/id #uuid "840f7119-572d-50c5-bbbf-83c0cac108a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c42bc75b2c46989d81d20a9025ee8e487408f06576ec4a00115f20891cf5a824.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393559} {:answer/id #uuid "40d3edc5-becc-58a4-934d-50f6796ae78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09b44673879a2991acb4d5cdba4fad6233a87035b51cd9aa090ee9445ba5fa42.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393560} {:answer/id #uuid "95376528-e4b3-531b-86b5-3f2b9c559f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/47f95dca66badab7adf57a36680877109c2d918210a1a412fd4e6532d973cd61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393561} {:answer/id #uuid "d5663d4b-636b-5cde-93d7-c48645e55519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d4d87620b1b1f7ec62825bbd532998595455f601db2ba7c841e0a0167dff4fb4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d82492-3855-57ba-b3fa-259a3e6e9203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c0/c0bffb825fa5610288e3c88555a41c8124cf3538b2f75ebe627543a678ca3fba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393558} :answer-field/id #uuid "d944ba04-8e3b-5f93-b26b-2c144908ea48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9be8ba94-ab90-5127-ba65-2ca25adc7b85" :question/math-academy-id "q-75737" :question/problem "![](images/53/539b08ea12f9af52defd826c0b63c194f36b6de408770c1bc1975bd6ca84724f.png)\n\nThe vector $u$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the vector $Bu.$" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvector $u=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Bu & =-\\frac{1}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](images/c0/c0bffb825fa5610288e3c88555a41c8124cf3538b2f75ebe627543a678ca3fba.png)"}]}
